{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"First, we note that $z=-\\dfrac12$ makes the denominator zero and so cannot be a solution.\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2z+1$ to get rid of the quotient. ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{z - 6}{2z + 1} \u0026= 10\n\\\\\\\\\n(2z+1)\\cdot \\dfrac{z - 6}{2z + 1} \u0026= (2z+1)\\cdot 10\n\\\\\\\\\nz-6\u0026=20z+10\n\\\\\\\\\n-16\u0026=19z\n\\\\\\\\\n-\\dfrac{16}{19}\u0026=z\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We get that $z=-\\dfrac{16}{19}$ is a potential solution. It doesn't make the denominator zero, so it is indeed a solution.\n\n**In conclusion, $z=-\\dfrac{16}{19}$.**","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Solve for $z$.**\n\n$\\dfrac{z - 6}{2z + 1} = 10$\n\n$z =$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"answerForms":["proper","improper"],"maxError":0,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":-0.8421052631578947}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
